arXiv:2509.09790v1 [cs.AI] 11 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuxuan Li
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>David R. Cheriton School of Computer Science
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Waterloo
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Waterloo, ON, N2L 3G1, Canada
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yxuuan.li1@uwaterloo.ca</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Victor Zhong
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>David R. Cheriton School of Computer Science
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Waterloo
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Waterloo, ON, N2L 3G1, Canada
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">victor.zhong@uwaterloo.ca</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning to plan in grounded environments typically requires carefully designed reward functions or high-quality annotated demonstrations.
Recent works show that pretrained foundation models, such as large language models (LLMs) and vision language models (VLMs), capture background knowledge helpful for planning, which reduces the amount of reward design and demonstrations needed for policy learning.
We evaluate how well LLMs and VLMs provide feedback across symbolic, language, and continuous control environments.
We consider prominent types of feedback for planning including binary feedback, preference feedback, action advising, goal advising, and delta action feedback.
We also consider inference methods that impact feedback performance, including in-context learning, chain-of-thought, and access to environment dynamics.
We find that foundation models can provide diverse high-quality feedback across domains.
Moreover, larger and reasoning models consistently provide more accurate feedback, exhibit less bias, and benefit more from enhanced inference methods.
Finally, feedback quality degrades for environments with complex dynamics or continuous state spaces and action spaces.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training instruction following agents in grounded environments often requires a large number of trials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mnih et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> or a large set of high-quality annotated demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stepputtis et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mandlekar et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Recent progress in large-scale pretraining shows that large language models (LLMs) capture a significant amount of world-knowledge that are helpful for reasoning and planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
While these models struggle as direct policies in grounded environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mandlekar et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, they can provide helpful judgment and feedback on agent behaviour.
For instance, LLMs are able to generate and refine executable reward functions with human feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as well as identify productive behaviour for imitation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Colas et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a comprehensive study on how well LLMs and VLMs can provide accurate feedback in grounded environments.
Given a LLM/VLM-based Feedback Model (FM), a language instruction, observation from the environment, and agent behaviour, we compare FM feedback on the agent behaviour to ground truth feedback derived from an optimal policy.
We evaluate state-of-the-art FMs across model sizes, reasoning capabilities, multiple domains, observation modality, and inference methods.
First, we consider feedback types commonly used in planning: <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">binary feedback</span> on whether the action is optimal; <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">preference</span> on which action out of a pair results in faster task completion; <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">action advising</span> to choose the correct action; <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">goal advising</span> to identify the next subgoal; and <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">delta action</span> to provide action correction.
Second, we consider a diverse selection of domains ranging from symbolic games, verbalized text environments, and continuous control of a robotic arm.
Third, we evaluate how the quality of feedback changes under inference methods that affect model performance, such as observation modality, hints about domain dynamics, in-context learning, and chain-of-thought.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We find that Feedback Models can achieve high feedback accuracy across domains.
First, feedback quality improves with larger and reasoning models.
The latter exhibit less class and positional bias, and benefit the most from test-time techniques such as hints about domain dynamics, in-context learning, and chain-of-thought.
Second, out of the feedback types we consider, FMs tend to provide high-quality binary and preference feedback more easily — this is especially true for non-reasoning models.
Third, feedback quality degrades in domains with complex, hierarchical dynamics, or domains with continuous state and action spaces.
On predominantly visual environments, we find that VLMs consistently outperform LLMs on preference and binary feedback in cases such as continuous control, where image observations are much easier to reason about than textual descriptions of state and action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, this is the first comprehensive analysis on how well LLMs and VLMs provide across diverse feedback types, domains, and inference methods.
We show that language model feedback is a promising means to extract learning signals for planning in a diverse selection of environments, without hand-engineered reward functions or human demonstrations.
Future research should investigate how to effectively elicit robust feedback, as well as how to learn from such feedback efficiently.
Given the success of high-quality language model feedback across diverse domains, we advocate for further research into how this feedback can be used to improve policy learning efficiency and effectiveness.
Additionally, we advocate for further research in how to extract high-quality feedback in domains with complex dynamics and continuous state and action spaces — features that make it particularly challenging to design rewards and label demonstrations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate how well LLMs and VLMs (which we will refer to as LMs) provide feedback in grounded environments.
Specifically, an LM serves as a <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feedback Model</span> (FM) that provides judgment on how well an agent is performing a task given the task instruction and snapshots of the agent’s behaviour.
Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents an overview of how we evaluate the Feedback Model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09790v1/language_model_feedback.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/238;" width="548" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Evaluation of action advising feedback as an example. We sample trajectories according to policy <math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, then combine snapshots <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> from trajectory with expert policy <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute ground truth feedback <math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, we prompt the Feedback Model and compare its response <math id="S2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> to the ground truth feedback <math id="S2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize feedback collection using terminology from sequential decision-making.
Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> denote the state space, <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> the action space, <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:S\times A\times S\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:S\times A\times S\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the transition function, <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:S\times A\times S\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:S\times A\times S\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the reward function, and <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> the discount factor.
We assume a Markov Decision Process <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\langle S,A,T,R,\gamma\ \rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\langle S,A,T,R,\gamma\ \rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where an agent acts according to policy <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}:S\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}:S\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>.
In planning, we find an optimal policy <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes expected cumulative discounted future reward <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\pi}[\sum_{t}\gamma^{t}R(s_{t},a_{t},s_{t+1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\pi}[\sum_{t}\gamma^{t}R(s_{t},a_{t},s_{t+1})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
To characterize how well a state maximizes <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct the value function <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\pi}[G|s_{0}=s]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\pi}[G|s_{0}=s]</annotation></semantics></math> and the state-action value function <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Q_{\pi}(s,a)=\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\pi}[G|s_{0}=s,a_{0}=a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\pi}(s,a)=\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\pi}[G|s_{0}=s,a_{0}=a]</annotation></semantics></math>.
In order to evaluate the Feedback Model, we assume access to an optimal policy <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Feedback Model is given <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, a <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">snapshot</span> consisting of policy behaviour and environment observations.
The FM then provides <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{F}(x)=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{F}(x)=y</annotation></semantics></math>, its <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">feedback</span> on the behaviour <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
We evaluate the correctness of the judgment by comparing it with <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ground truth feedback</span> <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> computed using the optimal policy.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Types of feedback</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider five types of feedback commonly used in planning.
For each, we describe the composition of snapshot <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and how the ground truth feedback <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Binary feedback.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FM observes a snapshot containing the state and an action <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> and provides a binary judgment <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{F}(x)={y_{\rm bin}}\in\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>bin</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{F}(x)={y_{\rm bin}}\in\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> representing whether the action <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is optimal in state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
The ground truth binary feedback is 1 if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(s)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(s)=a</annotation></semantics></math> and -1 otherwise.
For cases where there exist several optimal actions, we consider <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\rm bin}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>bin</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\rm bin}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be correct if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is any of the optimal actions.
Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Domains and Dataset collection details ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> details how we compute <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for different domains.
Binary feedback estimates binarized advantage value <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\pi}(s,a)=Q_{\pi}(s,a)-V_{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\pi}(s,a)=Q_{\pi}(s,a)-V_{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pérez-Dattari et al. (2020)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warnell et al. (2018)</a></cite> use binary feedback to directly learn policies.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al. (2024)</a></cite> use binary feedback to identify productive behaviour for imitation learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Action advising.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FM directly provides the optimal action given the state presented in the snapshot <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=s</annotation></semantics></math>.
The ground truth feedback is the output of the optimal policy <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\rm act}}^{*}=\pi^{*}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>y</mi><mi>act</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\rm act}}^{*}=\pi^{*}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For cases where there exist several optimal actions, we consider <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\rm act}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>act</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\rm act}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be correct if it is any of the optimal actions.
LLM has been used to predict optimal actions for long-horizon games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, robotic control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and computer use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preference feedback.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FM observes a snapshot consisting of the state and a pair of potential actions <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(s,a_{1},a_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(s,a_{1},a_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, and chooses the action more conducive for solving the task (e.g. higher value <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>).
The ground truth feedback uses <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> under the optimal policy in order to compute the ground truth preference:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\rm pref}}^{*}=\begin{cases}+1,&amp;\text{if }\ Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{1})&gt;Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{2})\\
-1,&amp;\text{if }\ Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{1})&lt;Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{2})\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>y</mi><mi>pref</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\rm pref}}^{*}=\begin{cases}+1,&amp;\text{if }\ Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{1})&gt;Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{2})\\
-1,&amp;\text{if }\ Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{1})&lt;Q_{\pi^{*}}(s,a_{2})\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2021)</a></cite> use human preference to learn a reward model for reinforcement learning.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2023)</a></cite> also uses human preferences to learn a preference function, with which they pseudo label data for policy improvement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Goal advising.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Goal advising is similar to action advising in that the FM is given a snapshot consisting of the state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=s</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, instead of predicting the next action, the FM predicts the ideal next state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to best solve the task (e.g. higher value <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(s^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The ground truth feedback is computed using the value function under the optimal policy <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\pi^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\pi^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\rm goal}}={\mathrm{argmax}}_{s^{\prime}\in{\mathrm{Reachable}}(s)}V_{\pi^{*}}(s^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>goal</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Reachable</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\rm goal}}={\mathrm{argmax}}_{s^{\prime}\in{\mathrm{Reachable}}(s)}V_{\pi^{*}}(s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, we use the transition function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to define <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Reachable}:s\rightarrow\{s^{\prime}\in S\mid\exists a\in A,T(s,s^{\prime},a)&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Reachable</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reachable}:s\rightarrow\{s^{\prime}\in S\mid\exists a\in A,T(s,s^{\prime},a)&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> as a mapping from a state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to a set of states with non-zero transition probability from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there are multiple optimal next states, we consider <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\rm goal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>goal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\rm goal}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be correct if it is any of the optimal next states.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2023)</a></cite> use LLMs to generate subgoals and give intermediate rewards to the agent when it achieves the generated subgoals.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2024)</a></cite> similarly decompose goals using LLMs, however the low-level goals are then further given to policy LLMs to predict the appropriate action.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Delta action feedback.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FM is given a snapshot consisting of the state and an action <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, and provides a corrective delta action <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is more conducive for solving the task than <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g.higher value <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s,a+\Delta)&gt;Q(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s,a+\Delta)&gt;Q(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>).
The ground truth feedback uses the optimal policy in order to compute the ground truth delta action <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\Delta}}^{*}=\pi^{*}(s)-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\Delta}}^{*}=\pi^{*}(s)-a</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\Delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\Delta}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be correct if it is within some threshold <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of the ground truth <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{\Delta}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{\Delta}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a></cite> map verbal feedback provided by human observers to a set of delta actions, which are used to correct actions produced by a policy.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Colas et al. (2023)</a></cite> convert agent trajectories to language descriptions, and use LLMs to retroactively provide corrections to actions in the trajectory.</p>
</div>
