</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Task Domains</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to measure how well Feedback Models provide feedback across diverse settings, we collect policy behaviours from several distinct domains that vary in observation space, action space, degree of observability, and task settings.
Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Delta action feedback. ‣ 2.1 Types of feedback ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates these domains, which include symbolic games CliffWalking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton, 2018</a>)</cite> and MiniGrid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chevalier-Boisvert et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, navigation and high-level manipulation text simulators ALFWorld <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shridhar et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and HierchyCraft <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Federico, 2023</a>)</cite>, and continuous control Robomimic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mandlekar et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
We evaluate LLM feedback for all domains, and VLM feedback for predominantly visual domains (MiniGrid Door Key and Robomimic).
Appendix  <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Verbalisation and Prompt Examples ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> shows examples of FM prompts for each environment.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cliff Walking</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent navigates to a fixed goal location in a grid of size 4x12 without falling off the cliff located in the last row.
The agent has full observability of the grid, along with its current position (e.g. <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(3, 0)</span>), and chooses among the actions <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">right</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">left</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">up</span>, and <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">down</span> to navigate to the goal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MiniGrid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chevalier-Boisvert et al., 2023</a>)</cite></h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider two tasks where the agent navigates between rooms in order to reach a goal location.
In Four Rooms (Fig <a href="#S2.F2.sf5" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Delta action feedback. ‣ 2.1 Types of feedback ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(e)</span></a>), the agent needs to navigate four rooms to reach the goal.
It chooses among the actions <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">turn left</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">turn right</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">move forward</span>.
In Door Key (Fig <a href="#S2.F2.sf4" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Delta action feedback. ‣ 2.1 Types of feedback ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(d)</span></a>), the agent must first pick up the key, then unlock the door, then reach the goal.
The agent chooses among actions <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">turn left</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">turn right</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">move forward</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pick up</span>, and <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unlock door</span>.
For both tasks, the agent has full observability of the room layout and content.
We evaluate VLM feedback on Minigrid Door Key, as it involves more visual understanding components like locked door, unlocked door, wall and key.
Here, we use a screenshot of the current grid as the visual observation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">HierarchyCraft <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Federico, 2023</a>)</cite></h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the task of finding a diamond.
The agent must navigate to appropriate locations to collect materials (e.g. iron, wood) and craft items (e.g. iron pick axe) helpful for procuring the diamond.
The action space consists of 201 discrete actions (e.g. searching for wood in the forest).
The agent observes its inventory and the items in the environment via text description.
This environment is partially observable, as objects in a location are hidden by default.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ALFWorld <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shridhar et al., 2021</a>)</cite></h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent navigates and performs high-level interactions with objects in a house in order to accomplish high-level tasks (e.g. wash mugs and put them in the cabinet).
The agent observes the goal and text descriptions of the scene, and chooses among a list of valid actions described in language.
This environment is partially observable, as objects in a location are hidden by default (e.g. cups in a cabinet).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robomimic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mandlekar et al., 2021</a>)</cite></h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the lift object task, where the agent controls a 7-DoF robot arm to lift an object.
The agent has full observability of the end effector position, the object position.
It chooses from a list of control vectors that move the end effector and adjust the gripper state.
Similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mandlekar et al. (2021)</a></cite>, we assume an external policy that can accurately control the gripper rotation.
In our experiments, we represent the observations via text description and provide a list of delta actions for the agent to choose from.
For VLM feedback, we use a single image instead of a text description of the current observation.
The image is captured from a camera in front of the desk and robot arm, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2.sf6" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Delta action feedback. ‣ 2.1 Types of feedback ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(f)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
