<td id="S2.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3000</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">500</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">1500</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Robomimic (Lift)</th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2015</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2015</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">2015</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">ALFWorld</th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2778k</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3942</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">105k</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">MineCraft</th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">139k</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1000</td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">11k</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Dataset size for each domain and feedback type.
The listed types are preference feedback, action advising, and binary feedback.
Delta action is supported in Robomimic with 2015 examples.
Goal advising is supported in Cliff Walking with 37 examples.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Dataset Collection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each domain, we collect a dataset of policy behaviour <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{x_{1},x_{2}\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{x_{1},x_{2}\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math> by sampling from a policy <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each snapshot <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first compute the set of optimal actions using the optimal policy <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, then compute the ground truth feedback <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For Cliff Walking, we enumerate all states/ actions for a dataset of 37 snapshots for action/goal advising, 148 for binary feedback, and 444 for preference feedback.
For other domains, we use 3 choices of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>: expert <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, random policy that uniformly samples actions, and half-expert which alternates between the two with 50% chance.
For each state, we enumerate all actions to construct <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, for Four rooms, we sample <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> states <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\ldots s_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\ldots s_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and create a state-action pair for each of the 3 actions to build a binary feedback dataset of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3N</annotation></semantics></math> examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We cannot compute the optimal actions for Robomimic Lift Object, as it is impossible to enumerate all possible continuous actions.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mandlekar et al. (2021)</a></cite>, we instead discretize the action space using a threshold based on empirical evaluation of a behavioural cloning expert, and use this threshold to enumerate the set of delta actions.
The size of the collected dataset is shown in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Robomimic ( , ) ‣ 2.2 Task Domains ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Feedback Conditions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across each domain, we evaluate FM inference methods that are known to affect LLM performance in grounded environments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">In-Context Learning (ICL)</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ICL improves LLM performance by providing input and output examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, from which LLMs deduce patterns that help generalize to new tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In this setting, we combine instructions, observations, and ground truth feedback as ICL examples for the FM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Chain-of-Thought and Thinking Guides (CoT)</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CoT techniques encourage LLMs to generate intermediate steps towards the final solution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and consistently improve performance for reasoning problems such as program synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
We incorporate CoT in all experiments.
In this additional setting, we design planning-specific questions, called <span id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Thinking Guides</span>, for the FM to answer to encourage the completion of helpful high-level intermediate steps (e.g. reasoning about its inventory).
Instead of trying to deduce the reasoning procedure from ICL examples, Thinking Guides directly gives FMs high-level hints for how to reason.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Access to High-level Domain Dynamics</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaiser et al. (2020)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al. (2023)</a></cite> show that knowledge of environment dynamics improve model-based reinforcement learning.
In this setting, we enumerate the transition dynamics of the environment and either provide a full description of the dynamics or a limited partial version explaining only legal actions.
We additionally explore a second setting, where instead of directly providing environment dynamics, we provide <span id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">history context</span> to the FM model.
This evaluates the FM’s ability to infer relevant dynamics from the history.
In order to provide history context for Cliff Walking, we use an expert policy to sample trajectories.</p>
</div>
