<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>
Feedback accuracy for <span id="S2.T2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFB3B3;">Llama 3.1 8B</span>, <span id="S2.T2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#B3B3FF;">Llama 3.1 70B</span>, <span id="S2.T2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#B3FFB3;">QwQ 32B</span>, and <span id="S2.T2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFB3;">R1</span>.
Delta action is only applicable for continuous state spaces (Robomimic), its feedback column is not shown due to space constraint — Robomimic Delta Action numbers are 0.07 for Llama 3.1 8B, 0.12 for Llama 3.1 70B, 0.03 for QwQ, and 0.07 for R1.
For goal advising, we only consider cases ground truth feedback is computable — there exists an intermediate state that the FM can propose which the optimal policy must encounter (Cliff Walking).
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments and Analyses</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design experiments to quantify how well LMs provide feedback across different feedback types, environment domains, observation modality, and inference methods.
We conduct our study with LLama 3.1 7B, Llama 3.1 70B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, QwQ 32B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2024</a>)</cite>, and DeepSeek R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
The Llama models are non-reasoning models, while QwQ and R1 are reasoning models with performance similar to the best closed-source models.
We evaluate VLM feedback with Qwen 2.5 VL 7B, Qwen 2.5 VL 32B, Qwen 2.5 VL 72B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and GPT o4 mini.
We show Feedback Model prompts in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Verbalisation and Prompt Examples ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for each feedback type and for each feedback condition.
We consider malformatted feedback and feedback with illegal values to be incorrect.
During the evaluation, we down-sample the large datasets for ALFWorld and Robomimic to 1000.
For preference feedback, we only evaluate feedback on pairs of optimal and non-optimal actions, as opposed to pairs of equally optimal actions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The accuracy of Feedback Models across domains and feedback types under the expert policy is shown in Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Access to High-level Domain Dynamics ‣ 2.4 Feedback Conditions ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
We show results on snapshots from expert policy, except for the smaller Cliff Walking domain where we enumerate all possible states.
Additional results using random policies and mixed policies are shown in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Results under different distributions ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLMs can provide high-quality feedback</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Access to High-level Domain Dynamics ‣ 2.4 Feedback Conditions ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that LLMs can provide accurate feedback across domains.
Moreover, larger models and reasoning models provide more accurate feedback regardless of feedback type, across different domains.
For instance, the smaller QwQ consistently outperforms the larger non-reasoning Llama 3.1 70B.
One source of small FM errors is that small FMs are prone to bias.
First, Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Positive vs Negative Bias in Binary Feedback ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> show that they are biased towards predicting actions as non-optimal, resulting in much worse recall than precision for binary feedback.
Second, Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Positional bias in pairwise preferences ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> show that they are biased towards preferring the first action, resulting in much worse accuracy when the ground truth action is located second for preference feedback.
In contrast, larger FMs exhibit far less positive/negative bias in binary feedback and positional bias in preference feedback.</p>
</div>
