<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>
VLM Feedback accuracy for <span id="S3.T5.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFB3B3;">Qwen 2.5 VL 7B</span>, <span id="S3.T5.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#B3B3FF;">Qwen 2.5 VL 32B</span>, <span id="S3.T5.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#B3FFB3;">Qwen 2.5 VL 72B</span>, and <span id="S3.T5.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFB3;">o4-mini</span>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preference and Binary Feedback are easier to provide</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference and Binary Feedback are generally the easiest forms of feedback to accurately provide across domains, especially for non-reasoning models.
For instance, Llama 3.1 8B and 70B provide significantly more accurate binary feedback and preference feedback than action advising.
In contrast, larger models such as QwQ and R1 are able to provide action advising with high quality similar to binary and preference feedback.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image observation allows better feedback in continuous domains</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VLM feedback results are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ LLMs can provide high-quality feedback ‣ 3 Experiments and Analyses ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
For predominantly visual domains, VLMs do not necessarily outperform LLMs if one can verbalize the environment succinctly.
For example, in the Minigrid Door Key, we observed that even one of our best models, Qwen 2.5VL 72B shows worse performance than the model with similar sizes (Llama 3 70B) in text feedback.
However, for Robomimic, in which it is difficult to provide succinct descriptions of states and actions, VLMs give preference up to 82% accuracy and binary feedback up to 62% accuracy, outperforming even bigger LLMs (see Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Access to High-level Domain Dynamics ‣ 2.4 Feedback Conditions ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Here, text observation requires a deep understanding of mechanical dynamics, while image observation is more intuitive to understand and hence gives higher accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feedback quality degrades significantly in continuous state/action spaces</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Access to High-level Domain Dynamics ‣ 2.4 Feedback Conditions ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that in the Robomimic continuous control domain, no model is able to provide accurate feedback.
We observe similar patterns for VLMs, with the exception of preference feedback.
<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            OLAF  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> similarly extract language feedback for Robomimic, however unlike in OLAF, humans do not facilitate querying feedback in our experiments.</span></span></span>
We additionally experiment with egocentric verbalization, which has been shown to improve LLM planning in embodied environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shridhar et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schumann et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, however experiments in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Egocentric Observation ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> show that this does not significantly improve performance of any FMs.
Our results suggest that current foundation models struggle with interpreting continuous state and action spaces without human prior knowledge.</p>
</div>
