<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Feedback accuracy with the FM not given domain dynamics (Unk), given fully domain dynamics via text description (Full), given domain dynamics only for legal actions in the current state (Legal Action), and inferring dynamics from history context (History). For Robomimic, the last column is Delta action. For CliffWalking, the last column is Goal Advising.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ICL and Thinking Guides respectively help bigger and reasoning models</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ LLMs can provide high-quality feedback ‣ 3 Experiments and Analyses ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ LLMs can provide high-quality feedback ‣ 3 Experiments and Analyses ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show performance gains from ICL and Thinking Guides.
Smaller FMs generally do not benefit from either techniques and are occasionally confused by the additional complexities in the prompt.
For instance, Llama 3.1 8B degrades by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.1 across feedback types in Door Key with ICL, and by as much as 0.21 in HierarchyCraft with Thinking Guides.
In contrast, for larger FMs, ICL gains tend to be consistently positive, with negative changes being very limited.
For instance, in HeirarchyCraft, ICL improves action advising of R1 by 0.1 and preference of Llama 3.1 70B by 0.42.
Thinking Guides consistently improve the performance of reasoning FMs such as QwQ and R1, though by less than ICL.
On non-reasoning models, its gains are inconsistent across feedback types.
For VLMs, neither ICL nor Thinking Guides consistently improves performance, apart from ICL binary feedback for small VLMs in Qwen 2.5 VL 7B and 32B.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Access to environment dynamics hints can improve feedback accuracy.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Feedback quality degrades significantly in continuous state/action spaces ‣ 3 Experiments and Analyses ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows FM performance given different hints about high-level domain transition dynamics.
Full descriptions of dynamics generally improve feedback quality, especially for small domains such as CliffWalking and Door Key.
For complex domains such as HierarchyCraft, long prompts that describe complex dynamics degrade the performance of small FMs, but consistently improve the performance of large FMs.
For instance, the 8B Llama feedback worsens across feedback types while the 70B model consistently improves, by as much as 0.24 for preference feedback. Similar trends hold when FMs induce dynamics from history context: large models benefit from context while small models do not.
A more significant improvement is observed in VLM feedback. Allowing access to history context allows even up to 22% increase in action advising accuracy in Minigrid Doorkey and 44% increase on binary feedback accuracy in Robomimic. In Door Key, feedback accuracy with history context and unknown dynamics shows similar performance with fully known dynamics, suggesting that the VLM is able to infer dynamics from the history context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Analysis of wrong responses</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify main types of feedback errors as <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">format</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">insufficient knowledge</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">incorrect reasoning</span>, and <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">incorrect visual understanding</span> and show detailed examples in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Feedback Model Error Examples ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Format</span> errors overwhelmingly occur with small FMs, which are are prone to output invalid JSON and invent answers not in the given options (e.g <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BOTH</span> during preference feedback).
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insufficient knowledge</span> errors occur when FMs lack the world knowledge necessary to deduce the correct judgment.
This type of error occurs most in complex domains such as HierarchyCraft, where FMs express incorrect assumptions about the domain, and reason (correctly) towards the wrong conclusion.
A less frequent source of such errors is from powerful FMs that make extraneous assumptions about the domain.
For example, an action is preferable in Cliff Walking if it reduces the number of steps to the goal, yet R1 sometimes misclassifies optimal actions because R1 deems it important to maintain a long distance from the cliff.
Giving the FMs hints about domain dynamics reduces the frequency of these errors.
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect reasoning</span> errors occur when the FM demonstrate a correct understanding of domain dynamics, but incorrectly reasons towards a wrong conclusion.
This is common in domains with complex state spaces such as continuous sensors in Robomic, where FMs tend to correctly state domain dynamics but incorrectly calculate state transitions.
For instance, the FM may calculate the incorrect end effector location in Robomic after taking a delta action despite knowing the formula, or picking up unnecessary items in HierarchyCraft despite knowing the correct recipes. <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect visual understanding</span> happens when the vision language model cannot correctly understand the image observation. For example, in Door Key, weaker VLMs frequently misinterpret the orientation of the agent and the location of walls.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning from feedback</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning from feedback pre-dates LLMs.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ross et al. (2011)</a></cite> directly uses expert demonstrations to learn policies.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho &amp; Ermon (2016)</a></cite> induce policies from offline expert behaviour, without interacting with experts during training.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Torabi et al. (2018)</a></cite> induce policies from observations in expert trajectories, without access to annotated expert actions.
Instead of direct policy induction, expert behaviour can also be used to infer reward functions in inverse reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ng &amp; Russell, 2000</a>)</cite>, which can then be used to train policies.
Instead of expert behaviour, several recent works instead use expert preference to infer reward functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Metcalf et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Vision language models have also been used to provide preference for RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Compared to prior work, we evaluate LLM feedback without expert policies and without annotated demonstrations.
Extracting high quality automatic feedback significant reduces the amount of human labour required for training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning from AI feedback</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of extracting feedback from domain experts, recent work extract feedback from domain-agnostic pretrained foundation models.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2022)</a></cite> outline a framework for alignment using AI feedback during pretraining and fine-tuning of LLMs.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2023)</a></cite> use LLMs to generate intermediate goals for reinforcement learning.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2023)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite> generate reward functions respectively using LLMs and vision-language models.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Colas et al. (2023)</a></cite> use LLMs to retroactive relabel actions.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al. (2024)</a></cite> use LLMs to identify desirable behaviours for imitation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Several works use LLMs to generate preference data for exploration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Klissarov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and policy search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al. (2024)</a></cite> critically evaluate this last family of preference approaches, noting that gains largely result from combining superior teachers and weaker students.
Our work is a unified, comprehensive study of AI feedback approaches, specifically evaluating the limitations of current state-of-the-art LLMs for providing planning feedback in grounded environments.
We consider the primary types AI feedback in prior works, and evaluate these techniques on a shared, diverse set of domains across model classes and inference methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLMs for planning</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs capture world knowledge helpful for planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stamper et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a></cite> use LLMs as policy for learning Minecraft.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al. (2022)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vemprala et al. (2024)</a></cite> use LLMs to compose executable code using predefined APIs for robotic control.
These are but a small representative sample recent works that use LLMs as a policy.
LLMs can also be used to provide action priors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, subgoals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and reward functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for reinforcement learning.
These methods for using LLMs in planning are two specific types of feedback considered in our study.
The action advising feedback is identical to using LLM as policy.
The goal advising feedback is similar to using LLM for next-goal generation or value estimation.
Beyond action and goal advising, we evaluates how well LLMs can provide other types of feedback under diverse inference methods and environment settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion and future work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a comprehensive study on evaluating language model feedback across feedback types, environment domains, and inference methods.
We find that LLM feedback quality improves with larger models and reasoning models, with large reasoning models providing diverse feedback with 80+% accuracy for most domains.
These models also exhibit less class and positional bias.
Inference-time techniques such as in-context learning, chain-of-thought, and dynamics hints improve the performance of large reasoning models, however the complex prompt injections they require degrade performance of smaller or non-reasoning models.
For domains such as continuous control, where it is challenging to provide succinct textual descriptions of state and actions, VLMs can provide higher quality feedback than LLMs.
Given the evidence of high-quality LLM feedback, we encourage future research in how to leverage such feedback to systematically improve planning sample efficiency and quality.
In addition, future study is required in how to improve LLM feedback in domains with complex dynamics and with continuous state and action spaces — both common characteristics of challenging environments for which it is even more difficult to engineer rewards and label demonstrations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Though currently we only evaluate LLM feedback quality in grounded domains, the released feedback dataset can be used to train decision-making policies.
This feedback dataset can be misused (e.g. training to reinforce incorrect feedback) to mislead policy training or create misleading feedback models, both of which may result in harmful model behaviour if deployed.
We consider this work as a first step towards understanding LLM feedback, such that we can quantify limitations and mitigate risks when using such feedback to train models with less human annotation and fewer labelled demonstrations.</p>
</div>
</section>
