<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Egocentric Observation</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labash et al. (2020)</a></cite> show that egocentric observation ability as well as perspective-taking ability is important for policy learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labash et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
For LLMs specifically, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Szot et al. (2023)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al. (2024)</a></cite> show that egocentric verbalization improves the performance of LLM agents in embodied planning.
Table <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix A Egocentric Observation ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that unlike prior results for planning, egocentric verbalization generally degrades feedback performance across feedback types and across most domains.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T7" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A1.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
