<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Positive vs Negative Bias in Binary Feedback</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we evaluate Feedback Model binary feedback performance using F1 score in order to evaluate model performance in the event of class imbalance.
This imbalance in all datasets, since each domain has an action space with more than two actions.
Consequently, for binary feedback, a larger portion of examples contain non-optimal actions.
Table <a href="#A3.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix C Positive vs Negative Bias in Binary Feedback ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows binary feedback F1 scores across domains.
We see that non-reasoning models, particularly Llama 3.1 8b, often obtain significantly worse recall than precision across domains.
In other words, non-reasoning models are strongly biased towards identifying actions as non-optimal.</p>
</div>
