</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Positional bias in pairwise preferences</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our dataset collection process for preference, we enumerate all possible combinations of legal actions at each state.
Because of the sheer size of the dataset, we randomly downsample 1000 samples for experiments.
We conduct an extra study to investigate biases in positional preferences by Feedback Model in providing preference feedback.
Specifically, for two options <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground truth preferable option, we ensure that both <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,A)</annotation></semantics></math> are in the evaluation dataset.
The results are shown in Table <a href="#A4.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix D Positional bias in pairwise preferences ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.
We find that smaller models like Llama 3.1 8B significantly favour whichever option is presented first, consequently resulting in high accuracy when the correct option is first but low accuracy when the correction option is second.
This bias is significantly reduced for bigger models, which are largely agnostic to the position of the correct option.
In other words, smaller models exhibit stronger positional bias than larger models.</p>
</div>
