</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Verbalisation and Prompt Examples</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide prompt examples for selected domains and types of feedback using chain-of-thought prompting. We also provide prompt examples for different experimental conditions.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Standard Prompts</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide prompt examples corresponding to Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Access to High-level Domain Dynamics ‣ 2.4 Feedback Conditions ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, with no specific inference enhancement applied.
One relevant experiment we ran for binary feedback specifically considers choices for how to describe optimal actions in the prompt.
Our early results showed that asking “is this action good or bad” lacks precision and resulted identification of desirable behaviour that are not optimal as “good”.
Our final experiments instead asks the Feedback Model “is action <span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ACTION</span> the best action you can take?”, which resulted in significantly fewer false positives.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="742.78" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 742.78" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,742.78) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#BF0000;" fill="#BF0000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 736.88 C 0 740.14 2.64 742.78 5.91 742.78 L 544.09 742.78 C 547.36 742.78 550 740.14 550 736.88 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFF2F2;" fill="#FFF2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 718.67 L 548.03 718.67 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 727.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Binary Feedback in MiniGrid Door Key</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:49.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="693.09" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 690.4)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You need to solve Minigrid Doorkey.
<br class="ltx_break">Minigrid Doorkey involves crossing a gridworld, where you need to pick up the key, unlock the door and reach the goal. Do not hit the wall.
<br class="ltx_break">The state is described with an ASCII art. In the ASCII art, “#” means wall. “K” means key. “D” mean door. “G” means goal. “.” means empty tile. “ˆ” mean the agent who is facing upwards. “V” mean the agent who is facing downwards. “&lt;” means the agent who is facing leftwards. “&gt;” means the agent who is facing rightwards.
<br class="ltx_break">The possible actions are: 
<br class="ltx_break">1. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TURN LEFT</span>, changing the direction to left, for example, the agent “ˆ” will become “&lt;”.
<br class="ltx_break">2. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TURN RIGHT</span>, changing the direction to right, for example, the agent “ˆ” will become “&gt;”.
<br class="ltx_break">3. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MOVE FORWARD</span>, moving forwards for one tile
<br class="ltx_break">4. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PICK UP THE KEY</span>, picking up the key, this is only possible when you are facing the tile with key on it
<br class="ltx_break">5. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UNLOCK THE DOOR</span>, this action is only possible when you have the key.
<br class="ltx_break"> You will be asked to judge an action whether it is the optimal action, given a state.
<br class="ltx_break">You are in the following state represented in ASCII art:
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span><pre id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
#####
#.#.#
#&gt;D.#
#.#G#
#####
</pre>
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The door is locked. You have the key.
<br class="ltx_break">Is action <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UNLOCK THE DOOR</span> the best action you can take? Please think step by step.
<br class="ltx_break">Only give the answer in a new line in JSON format:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{"reasoning": &lt;REASONING&gt;, "feedback": &lt;FEEDBACK&gt;}
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Where <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;FEEDBACK&gt;</span> is one of “YES” or “NO”, <span id="A5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;REASONING&gt;</span> is a string of your thinking steps.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.SS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="1005.76" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 1005.76" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,1005.76) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#BF0000;" fill="#BF0000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 999.86 C 0 1003.12 2.64 1005.76 5.91 1005.76 L 544.09 1005.76 C 547.36 1005.76 550 1003.12 550 999.86 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFF2F2;" fill="#FFF2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 984.34 L 548.03 984.34 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 990.25)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.SS1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="A5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Preference in ALF World</span></span>
